Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Warm period live-action, early twentieth-century English nursery; toys slightly animated within a live-action world

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Soft winter light through lace curtains. A child's stocking bulges on the bedpost. At the top, wedged charmingly, sits the VELVETEEN RABBIT — fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white velveteen, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: distant church bells, tissue paper rustling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse tumble from the stocking. THE BOY — about seven, fair hair, nightshirt — hugs the Rabbit tight, laughing.

(SOUND: child's delighted laugh, clockwork mouse whirring)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and Uncles crowd in with parcels. Tissue paper flies. In the excitement the Velveteen Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor, forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Months of soft light and dust motes. The Velveteen Rabbit sits alone on the floor near the toy cupboard. Expensive MECHANICAL TOYS glitter on a shelf; a MODEL BOAT with chipped paint; TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, stands stiff with importance.

(SOUND: nursery clock ticking, distant household murmur)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him. Being only made of velveteen, some of the more expensive toys quite snubbed him.

The mechanical toys turn their painted faces away. The Rabbit's thread whiskers droop.

Beside the fender lies the SKIN HORSE — very old, brown coat bald in patches showing seams, tail nearly bare of hairs. His glass eyes are kind.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Firelight on the fender. The Velveteen Rabbit and the Skin Horse lie side by side. Moonlight pools on the floorboards. The house is quiet.

(SOUND: low fire crackle, wind at the window)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept. Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles, gentle and certain.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs, longing and a little afraid. His pink sateen ears catch the firelight.

(MUSIC: soft, wondering theme, sparse piano)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Bedtime. THE BOY searches under chairs. NANA — stout, apron, practical hair pinned up — bustles, in a hurry.

(SOUND: drawers sliding, Nana's brisk footsteps)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear and pushes him into the Boy's arms. The Boy clutches him and is carried off.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A night-light burns on the mantelpiece. The Boy hugs the Rabbit very tight, rolls over on him, pushes him under the pillow. The Rabbit's whiskers bend; he can scarcely breathe.

(SOUND: soft bedclothes rustle, boy's steady breathing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

Later — Nana gone to supper — the Boy whispers, making tunnels under the bedclothes.

THE BOY
(whispering)
Burrows. Like real rabbits.

He falls asleep. The Rabbit snuggles under the Boy's warm chin. The Boy's hands stay clasped round him all night.

(SOUND: night-light soft hiss, distant house settling)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Time has passed. The Rabbit's velveteen is shabbier; his tail coming unsewn; pink rubbed from his nose where the Boy has kissed him. He does not notice. He only watches the sleeping Boy.

(SOUND: gentle night wind, clock chiming faintly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy — so happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring sun. The Boy trundles the Rabbit in a wheelbarrow, builds fairy huts under raspberry canes, digs burrows in the flower bed. The Rabbit rides and tumbles, earthy and loved.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow squeak, boy's laughter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and they had long days in the garden, for wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too.

Dusk. The Rabbit left alone on the lawn, wet with dew. Nana appears with a candle, grumbling, rubs him with her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Nana carries the damp Rabbit in. The Boy sits up in bed, arms stretched out.

(SOUND: bed springs, candle flame flutter)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit goes still in wonder. In his boot-button eyes — polish long gone — a look of wisdom and beauty gathers.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last. The nursery magic had happened to him. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

(MUSIC: warm resolving chord, quiet joy)

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Long June light. Bracken and tall trees near the house. The Boy settles the Rabbit in a little nest among the ferns, then wanders off to play.

(SOUND: wood pigeons, insects in grass, distant boy calling)

Ants run between the Rabbit's velvet paws. Two WILD RABBITS creep from the bracken — furry, brand-new, seams invisible, bodies changing shape as they move. Their noses twitch. They pad close.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways, stands on hind legs, whirls and dances.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can! Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit freezes. His back is all one piece, like a pincushion. He has no hind legs at all.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Wild Rabbit stretches his neck, looks hard.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

He dances until the little Rabbit grows dizzy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The Wild Rabbit comes so close his long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear — then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, jumps backward.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. The Boy runs past. With a stamp and a flash of white tails the wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

(SOUND: running feet, bracken thrashing, then sudden quiet)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants and swaying ferns answer. Little white moths flutter out as the sun sinks. The Boy returns and carries him home.

(MUSIC: thin, lonely air)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Weeks later. The Rabbit is very old and shabby — whiskers loved off, ear linings grey, brown spots faded, shape nearly gone. Still the Boy holds him close.

(SOUND: labored child breathing, restless sheets)

The Boy's face is flushed with fever. His little body burns against the Rabbit. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes, never stirring.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, one day, the Boy was ill. Through it all the little Velveteen Rabbit lay there, hidden from sight, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

He creeps to the pillow and whispers plans of gardens and raspberry thickets into the Boy's ear. At last the fever turns. Morning light. The Boy sits up with picture-books; the Rabbit cuddles at his side.

(SOUND: window sash raised, birds outside, relieved household voices)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - DAY

Bright sun. Windows wide open. The Boy, wrapped in a shawl, has been carried out to the balcony. The Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes, only his head peeping out.

(SOUND: summer breeze through curtains, distant garden work)

NANA and THE DOCTOR — black bag, serious coat — stand near the bed.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

THE DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes stare. Beyond the window, talk of the seaside tomorrow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hurrah, thought the little Rabbit. Tomorrow we shall go to the seaside!

But Nana's hands close around him.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

A sack, untied at the neck, slumped among old picture-books and rubbish at the end of the garden. Raspberry canes stand tall nearby like a jungle. The GARDENER digs potatoes in the distance, too busy for a bonfire until morning.

(SOUND: hens clucking, spade in earth, leaves rustling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so the little Rabbit was put into a sack with the old picture-books and a lot of rubbish, and carried out to the end of the garden behind the fowl-house.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Moon rising. The Rabbit wriggles until his shabby head pokes from the sack. He shivers; his coat is threadbare. He looks toward the raspberry thicket where he once played.

(SOUND: night insects, distant owl, faint shiver of leaves)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden — how happy they were — and a great sadness came over him. Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his little shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: single soft drop; then a strange hush)

Where the tear fell, a flower pushes from the earth — slender emerald leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. It opens. Out steps the NURSERY FAIRY — dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers at her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

(MUSIC: delicate chime, swelling wonder)

(SOUND: petals unfurling, soft chime of dew)

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I — I think I have seen you.

NURSERY FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

She holds him close and rises with him into the air, toward the wood.

EXT. WOOD GLADE - NIGHT

Moonlight. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass — then stop and stand in a ring as the Fairy descends.

(SOUND: soft thuds of dancing feet, then stillness; night breeze in leaves)

NURSERY FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit once more and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits frozen, shy, remembering his missing hind legs — and does not know the last kiss has already changed him.

Something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

He stares. He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny, instead of dingy velveteen. Ears that twitch by themselves. Whiskers long enough to brush the grass.

(SOUND: quick heartbeat of joy, grass whispering under new feet)

He leaps. He springs, jumps sideways, whirls as the others do, wild with the joy of those legs. When he finally stops to look for the Fairy, she is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

(MUSIC: bright, free running theme)

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Spring again. Warm sun. THE BOY — a little taller — plays among the trees behind the house.

(SOUND: birdsong, boy's footsteps in dry leaves)

Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through — and about his soft nose and round black eyes something familiar.

The Boy goes still, watching.

THE BOY
(softly, to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit holds his gaze a moment longer — then both rabbits slip back into the green.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The Boy runs on into the light. Among the bracken, a flash of soft brown fur, free and alive.

(SOUND: wind in the trees, distant happy shout, then quiet woodland peace)

(MUSIC: gentle reprise of the wondering theme, resolving)

FADE OUT.

THE END
